# Line endings are LF, everywhere, decided once.
#
# The repo already said this twice — .editorconfig (end_of_line = lf) and
# .prettierrc ("endOfLine": "lf") — but neither reaches git. With no attributes
# and core.autocrlf=false, git did NO translation in either direction, so
# whatever bytes a tool happened to write were the bytes that got committed.
#
# On Windows that is not hypothetical: PowerShell's Set-Content and a Node
# script using os.EOL both default to CRLF, and neither honors .editorconfig.
# On 2026-08-24 that turned 78-scheduling.prisma into a 1,149-line diff for a
# 7-line change, and db/src/index.ts into 617 lines for one added export —
# every other line "changed" only because its ending did.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` normalizes text on commit
# AND checks it out as LF. What it CANNOT do is rewrite a working file a tool
# just wrote with CRLF, so prettier can still flag one — this stops CRLF
# entering history and stops the phantom diffs, it does not police the tools.
*  text=auto  eol=lf

# Binary: never translated, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.avif   binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.mp4    binary
*.webm   binary
